            <ul>
                <li><strong>Atomization Dynamics:</strong> The sudden drop in pressure as the formulation exits the can causes the dissolved propellant to flash-boil, which provides the energy necessary to overcome the liquid's surface tension.</li>
                <li><strong>The Role of Propellants in Particle Distribution:</strong> Liquefied propellants like LPG (Liquefied Petroleum Gas) or DME (Dimethyl Ether) provide a constant internal pressure, ensuring the spray characteristics remain consistent from the first use to the final drop. Our <a href="/dimethyl-ether-dme-aerosol-propellant-guide/">DME propellant guide</a> covers the second in detail.</li>
                <li><strong>Valve and Actuator Design for Optimal Spray Patterns:</strong> Advanced actuators with specialized "swirl chambers" are essential for achieving the fine, misty dispersion expected in high-end air care products.</li>
                <li><strong>Viscosity and Surface Tension Management:</strong> The solvent system must be carefully tuned to allow for rapid breakup without compromising the stability of the fragrance oil within the formulation.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Technical Comparison of Dispersion Systems</h3>
            <p>
                When planning an air freshener product line, selecting the right propellant and valve combination is a central decision for the formulator. Each system offers distinct advantages in terms of cost, performance, and environmental impact. Below is a comparison of the most common systems used in the professional air care industry today.
            </p>
